WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.840
It's your anniversary?
With her?

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.240
You're straight?

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:06.120
Fuck off.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:10.080
Are you? With that tote bag?

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.320
Thank you for participating

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.640
in this lovely
social experiment.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.440
…so welcome
to Netflix: I Like to Watch!

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.880
We're here, we're queer.
Let's go, baby.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.680
-Hi, bitches.
-Oh, bitch.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.040
How are you?

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.000
Having these spaces
and these venues, like,

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.600
people seem
to take it for granted

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.920
when the reality
of the situation is…

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:41.360
Think about
how many queer venues,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.400
even before the lockdown,

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.040
were closing
and shutting down

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.520
and just couldn't fund
themselves.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.800
Without those spaces,
this show doesn't exist.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.240
That platform doesn't exist.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.000
You don't have
the entertainment

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:01.680
and you don't have
the safe spaces

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:02.960
that we all need.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.200
It's Netflix UK's
I Like to Watch

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.280
with none other than
the delicious The Vivienne

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:13.280
and the beautiful
Blu Hydrangea!

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:15.440
-One, two, three!
-Whoo!

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:16.800
Drag kings are
at the forefront

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:18.400
of the radical nature
of drag.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:19.440
But drag in and of itself

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:21.360
is, like, a radical,
political act, right?

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.160
And just at Stonewall

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:24.680
when it was
a trans woman at the front

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:26.720
and lesbians
that were, like, very key,

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.080
again,
people have been sidelined.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:29.440
And I think
we shouldn't forget

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.480
that there's
an entire community of drag

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:32.960
that we're not seeing
represented

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.480
in the mainstream media.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:37.880
Keep supporting drag
queens.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.080
Keep supporting
the divas of the dance.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:43.560
Just because a queen
hasn't been on Drag Race

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:46.360
doesn't mean they're less
valid than anybody else.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.360
Just check that…

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:04.960
-You look beautiful there.
-Oh, fab.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.440
Just some…
That's just working.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.560
Well, here we are,
back on the road,

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:10.560
back on tour,

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:11.800
back out with the girls.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.320
I'm so excited. I'm really,
actually buzzing.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.520
And it's not just, like,
any old show.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:17.440
We're just sitting,

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.200
watching some good telly
and some good movies,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.480
and just having
a good old bitch, really.

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:23.360
Yes.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.080
I did horse riding lessons
as a child.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.960
-Poor horse.
-And I kid you not…

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.680
Go on, tell me.
I want to hear the story.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.000
I'm still, like,
finding my feet

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.640
touring and travelling
post-"panny-dem".

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.800
Like, I've just been missing
human contact

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.880
and interactions and people.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.120
If I could summarise Chez's

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.720
vision when it comes
to a garment,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.520
it's literally

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.560
feathers, rhinestones
and a hope and a dream.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:56.160
And don't ask me to make it,

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.800
because we all know
how that turns out.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:00.320
One of the double edges
of the Drag Race sword

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.600
is that, you know,

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.440
people expect drag queens to
look like supermodels now,

00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:07.000
and that is
very far from the truth.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.400
You know, some of the best
queens in the country…

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.520
Miss Jason,

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.240
I mean,
she won't mind me saying,

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:13.800
she looks like your nan.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.560
But we will have
the best night ever

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:19.320
watching Miss Jason
in a bar in Brighton

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.320
than you maybe would

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.600
somebody who is
painted immaculately,

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.280
who's never been
on stage in their life.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:27.440
I don't care what you look
like as a drag queen.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.640
I would rather go and watch

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.680
a 60-year-old fella
in a bin bag

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.480
with blue eyeshadow
up to his eyebrows.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.040
As long
as they're entertaining,

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:37.400
I couldn't give a shit.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.320
I like to look nice.

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:42.160
But for me, that's not the
be-all and end-all of drag.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.200
When I go shopping,

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.040
I tend to get stuff
for me out of drag.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.320
So, like, really look after

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.440
the person
that's behind the mask.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.480
People often don't see
that side.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:56.280
And for me, I'm not very
much a character in drag.

00:03:56.360 --> 00:03:59.320
I'm just, like, me
with an IV of Red Bull.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.520
So, like,
I'm still the same person.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.280
But I also forget
that you are allowed

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:05.680
to look out for yourself.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.960
And I used to feel
tremendous guilt

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.400
every time I treated myself
to something out of drag,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:12.120
because I'm, like,
"This is money

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.120
that you should be putting
back into your work."

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.240
And it's, like,
actually, hold a second.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.320
You can treat yourself.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:19.640
Make some noise
for Cheryl Hole!

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:29.200
Very excited for tonight.
I can't wait.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.720
Been building it up
all week.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.720
It's, like,
"Ooh, it's nearly here."

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:34.840
And I haven't seen
The Vivienne

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:36.920
since before
she went on Ru Paul.

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:38.240
That was the last time
she was here,

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:39.960
just when it was announced.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.400
Thank you, Duncan.
I'm not paying for that.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.080
I'm gonna pop this here.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:47.600
A big thank you
to Duncan as well.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:48.760
He's the landlord.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.080
Give it up for Sundowners.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:54.160
I'm Duncan Bayles

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:55.800
and I own Sundowners.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:59.320
It's the only LGBTQ bar
in Margate

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.760
and for
quite an area around.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.400
Well, before I had this,

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.160
I was digging worms
on the beach.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:09.360
And the opportunity come up
to borrow some money

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:11.360
and have a change of career.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:12.960
I think you can
probably mention his name

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:14.800
to any of the bars,
restaurants around here

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.800
and they know exactly
who you're talking about.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:18.320
He is the kind of

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.240
beating heart of Sundowners
behind it all

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.800
and the true
kind of personality

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:22.840
that you kind of need,

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.000
not just the LGBT community
really locally.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:27.080
I think, kind of,
everyone sees Sundowners

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:29.680
as a kind of friendly local
kind of place.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.920
Anyone and everyone
kind of just comes in,

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.120
has a drink
and can socialise

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:34.240
with whoever they want.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.520
I felt through lockdown

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:36.920
there was times
when I didn't know

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.600
whether I wanted to carry on
or whether to walk away,

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.480
with what you're running up
in debts.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:44.160
It's, like,
how do you make it work?

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:45.040
Because the trouble is,

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.520
you didn't know how long
it was gonna go on for.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.240
We were closed in,
what was it, November 6th?

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:53.760
And we was told
it was four weeks,

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.520
and we didn't reopen
until the 12th April.

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:58.520
No one knew what
the future was gonna hold.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:00.320
They were, "We don't know
how we'll pay our bills.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.520
We don't know
how we'll do this, do that."

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:03.400
But we're not London.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:05.720
We haven't got a big scene

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:07.240
with money and that,
you know.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:09.720
If you were in London,

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.560
some of
the big bars there got

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:14.640
Arts Council funding
and stuff

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.360
that as a small, provincial
LGBT bar, we didn't get.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.720
With the amount of people
that have said since,

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.560
they were so worried

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:23.800
that we wouldn't survive

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:25.680
and they would have
nowhere to go after,

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.560
and that makes you realise
just how important you are

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:30.720
to the community.

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:34.760
The first night
we opened in July,

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.480
just to see people
being able to dance again.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.960
We'd spent all summer
telling people to sit down

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.320
every time they got up
to try and dance,

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:46.040
and to actually see people
get up and dance again

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:47.360
and hug each other,

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:51.000
I think… yeah, it turned
out it was quite amazing.

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:53.680
Quite emotional
in one way, that…

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:54.600
And people were.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:56.520
Margate, are you ready?

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.560
Yeah, I think we'll still be
here for a few more years.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:02.440
Can't afford to retire now.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.840
My God, look at Miles!

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:12.200
For a lot of drag queens,

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.600
it's the place
where they come into being.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:15.920
Um, it's the place

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:17.960
where you first discover
aspects about yourself.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:19.960
It's where you find
your freedom.

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:21.280
Um, they really are

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.400
the places where you can
start to explore

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:24.520
and play with identity,

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.200
but also start to explore

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:27.960
and play with creativity.

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:29.920
And it's through
that nexus of relationships

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:31.520
you find in smaller bars

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:33.960
that amazing things
are allowed to grow.

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:34.920
And I think it's the…

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.280
We all think of drag
in the big lights

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:38.840
and in the big cities.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:40.360
And I think the truth is,

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.280
to create really,
really interesting drag,

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:43.520
it has to be enmeshed

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:45.080
in that
kind of almost familial

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:46.360
community relationships.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:49.160
It takes a lot of love
to grow fabulous.

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:52.280
Now, can I just remind
you all he is 12 years old.

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:53.960
So, where's his…
Where's your mum?

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:55.800
Can I just say,
he is fabulous.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.520
Thank you so much
for being an amazing parent,

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.800
because the world needs
more allies.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:02.240
Honest to God.

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:05.760
There are children
and young trans people

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.280
and loads of people
from our community

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:09.280
who are getting ostracised
from their family

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:11.160
every single day.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:13.880
And for you to be such an
amazing, supportive parent

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:15.880
is really amazing,
so thank you.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.320
Miles has school tomorrow,
everybody.

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.400
Have you got school?

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.120
Oh, fuck off.
Can he call in sick?

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:32.560
Oh, the history
of the New Foresters

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:33.520
is absolutely amazing.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:36.760
It's, um, the oldest gay bar
in Nottingham,

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:38.360
going back to 1938.

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:42.400
Which is fantastic to find
that they've found evidence

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:43.880
of gay people
coming into here.

00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:46.840
A couple of weeks ago,
we had a marquee

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:49.320
outside in the car park,
because of the Covid.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:52.000
And I was watching over
the marquee

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.600
and I saw
this young lad crying.

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:56.240
He was only about 18, 19.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:08:59.680
And I saw all his friends,
all male friends,

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.400
round him
with their arms round him.

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:02.520
Now, I'm a landlady

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:05.000
who will go and communicate
with my customers

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:06.120
and make sure
that they're OK.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:08.120
So, I could see
that he wasn't very happy,

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:09.000
I thought.

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:11.120
So I went over
and basically asked him

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:13.400
and his mate says to me,
"Do you know something?

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:15.960
Because you've provided
this safe place,

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:18.200
he's just come out
to all his workmates."

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:20.560
And I've got no children,
so I was really proud.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.120
You know, I just thought,
you know,

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:23.880
we've… we've provided that.

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:25.000
And I thought
that was amazing.

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:26.440
That's the whole reason,
isn't it,

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:27.520
of us being here.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.960
Well, I moved to Liverpool
when I was 16.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:33.760
And I was working behind
a bar at 16, illegally.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:34.640
Um…

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:36.760
But it was
those queer venues

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:38.160
that Vivienne was born.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.400
You know, when you're young
and you come out,

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:41.280
and you feel…

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:43.360
You know, a lot of people
are kicked out at home,

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.120
so they've got
no family to turn to.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.320
So the fact that they
can come to places like this

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:48.320
and realise that,

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:50.040
"Oh, my God,
I'm actually gonna be fine.

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:51.720
All right, my family
have kicked me out,

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:54.200
but I've just found
a huge family,

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:57.320
globally, that are always
gonna have my back."

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:00.680
Nottingham!
Happy birthday…

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:02.520
To you

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:06.120
-Oh!
-Happy birthday

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:08.680
To you

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:10.000
Happy birthday…

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:12.520
It's a shit caterpillar
from Asda!

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:13.840
…dear Tia

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:15.320
-This hair is flammable.
-Watch your hair.

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:19.720
Happy birthday to you!

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:24.600
For health
and safety reasons…

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:27.640
-Come on, blow it!
-Blow! Blow it!

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:29.960
Aw, look at that.

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:34.680
I went to uni in Nottingham
and lived here

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:35.680
for five years,

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.840
so, uh, it's got a massive,
massive place in my heart

00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:40.400
and I'm really excited
to be back.

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:43.920
We are about to go into
the Nottingham New Theatre,

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:45.480
which is the only
student-run theatre

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:47.040
in England,
thank you very much.

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:49.160
I love it, it's
where I spent all my time

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:51.080
and learned all about,
sort of, performance

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:52.080
and things like that.

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:55.520
So, it's kind of had
a big impact on my life now

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:56.400
and my drag.

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:58.320
Wake up in the morning

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:00.040
Thinking we need more

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:02.200
We need more space
We need more storage

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:03.960
We need more support

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:06.600
We need events
To make societies

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:08.000
Open for all

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.400
Whether you live
On Sutton Bonington

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:12.000
Or in Sherwood Hall

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:13.080
I'm talking about…

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:15.080
-Hello.
-Hi there. Big hug.

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:17.720
-Lovely to meet you.
-Mucho. I'm Tia.

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:19.520
I'm the president
of the NNT currently.

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:21.160
Oh, lovely. That's nice.

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.440
You're the Lawrence Chaney
of the New Theatre?

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:25.000
-That's great.
-Thank you.

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:28.000
What, from your experience
of the NNT,

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:30.480
have you kind of carried
forward with you into drag?

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:32.960
Well,
I actually started drag

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:35.280
because I put on a musical

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:36.520
where there was
a drag role in it.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:37.400
Amazing.

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:39.560
And then I decided
I wanted to do that show,

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:41.360
to put it on myself
in London,

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:43.000
cast myself in the role,

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:45.680
um, and then
someone just said

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:47.720
that I should start doing it
and enter a competition.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:50.840
And then fast-forward
however many years,

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:53.480
and Drag Race and all of
that kind of thing.

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:55.480
Oh, but this is
the best moment.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:11:57.440
It's like all-singing,
all-dancing Eurovision

00:11:57.520 --> 00:11:59.480
with actual
Eurovision contestants.

00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:02.600
This is like…
my full idea of heaven.

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:05.800
I'm doing fine

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:08.120
All the things that I wanna…

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:11.320
-Welcome back.
-Oh, my goodness.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:14.640
-That's a separate room.
-I know.

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:16.800
It was only, like,
this bit before.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:20.680
Oh, this is great.
It's very well organised.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:23.160
OK, I'm not kidding.

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:28.760
I fully… This… That's why
I wanted to go in here.

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:30.680
I fully wrote my name
on one of these.

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:33.920
-And you've still got it.
-We've still got it.

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:35.520
We never throw
anything away.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:37.040
That's actually so mad.

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:38.080
I was, like, thinking

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:39.680
you must have
got rid of it by now.

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:41.160
But literally,
when I was leaving,

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:42.600
it was the last thing
that I did,

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:44.120
was write my name on this.

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:47.240
That's literally,
like, eight years ago.

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:48.120
That's so funny.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:49.800
I'm absolutely gonna have
to take a photo of it

00:12:49.880 --> 00:12:50.800
and send it to my friends.

00:12:50.880 --> 00:12:52.800
I know that's really sad,
because it's, like,

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:55.080
a lighting fixture.

00:12:55.160 --> 00:12:56.320
But, like,

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.360
they were literally

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:00.840
my whole life
while I was here.

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:04.640
That's really cute.
That's made me so happy.

00:13:05.200 --> 00:13:06.400
I think,
in my day-to-day life,

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:09.320
it can be very difficult
to sort of, like, maintain

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:10.600
that level of, like,

00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:14.320
positivity and, like,
joy within myself,

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:17.360
because, like, everyone has
struggles and difficulties,

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:18.520
especially

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:20.560
off the back of,
like, the lockdown

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:22.560
and everything
that we've gone through.

00:13:22.640 --> 00:13:24.680
Like, I think people are
massively underestimating

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:28.080
the mental health impact of,
like, the global pandemic

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:29.160
and everything
that's happened.

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:30.560
It's something
I really struggle with.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:33.040
I get so nervous
before I go on stage.

00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:35.160
I get really worried
that everyone's gonna think

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:36.720
that I look bad or sound bad

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:38.440
or all of
that kind of thing.

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:40.680
And, like,
it's easy for people

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:44.000
to say, like, "Oh whatever,
I do this, like, for me,"

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:45.120
and, like,
"My life is my own."

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:46.560
But also, like,
I absolutely don't.

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:49.280
Like, the reason why I do
drag is to entertain people.

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:50.920
So if I feel
like I'm bad at that,

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:53.680
that's gonna cause me,
like, massive insecurity.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:55.440
But I think, like,

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:57.440
the thing
that's been amazing for me

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:59.240
off the back of the show
is, like, getting this,

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:00.680
like, overwhelming
outpouring

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:03.240
of, like, love and so many
uplifting comments,

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:04.480
and things like that.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.760
It has made me feel
so much more self-assured

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:09.040
and given me
the ability to, like,

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:10.560
give that back to others.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:12.840
So this is probably
a little bit different

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:13.880
to how you remember.

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:16.160
-I mean, it's tidy…
-Well, yeah.

00:14:16.240 --> 00:14:17.560
-…which is nice.
-Thank you very much.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:19.200
It's really weird looking
at all these costumes

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:20.880
because, obviously,

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:24.200
costumes are
a massive part of my job,

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:26.200
especially preparing
for Drag Race.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:28.160
You have to spend
a lot of time,

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:30.520
and a lot of money,
on, um, outfits

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.480
that the judges may
or may not enjoy.

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:35.200
So, um, I wish I'd just come

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:36.640
back to the New Theatre,
to be honest.

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:37.720
Just nabbed a few things?

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:39.360
I could have worn a
football shirt, like Bimini

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:40.760
in the first episode.

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:42.720
Um, I think UK drag
has definitely changed

00:14:42.800 --> 00:14:43.880
in the last few years.

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:47.680
I feel like everyone,
sort of, leaned in more to…

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:49.720
I say everyone, I dressed
as an ice-cream cone.

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:50.720
I think a lot of people

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:52.720
have leaned more into the
fashion aspect of things.

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:54.080
I mean, like,
my drag mothers

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:55.360
and stuff like that are all

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:57.960
much more traditional,
sort of British

00:14:58.520 --> 00:14:59.600
cabaret,

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:01.200
uh, vibes.

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:03.280
I think if I could change
one thing about drag

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:04.600
in the UK in 2021,

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:06.800
I'd just wish people were
a bit funnier.

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:08.080
Do you know what I mean?

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:10.720
Death drops
aren't hilarious.

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:11.600
Do you know what is?

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:13.480
A 55-year-old
with shit blue makeup,

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:14.920
telling jokes on stage.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:16.400
Great. Love that.

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:21.400
My hometown

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:26.320
My hometown

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:48.480
I think

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:51.640
one of the frustrating
things that I see in drag

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:54.560
is the way that we are
sometimes held up

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:55.600
as these

00:15:56.080 --> 00:15:57.760
perfect images of beauty.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:15:58.920
And we're expected to be

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:00.680
these perfect images
of beauty

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:03.200
and ambassadors
of the beauty industry.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:05.320
And for me,
the real magic of drag

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:08.040
is the disruption
of the very idea

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:10.160
that something,
or one particular look,

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:12.040
is beautiful.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:14.280
I always say, the most
magical thing about drag

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:15.200
is taking it off.

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:17.040
And I'm very serious
about that.

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:18.680
Because
what makes drag special

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:21.160
and distinct from
other kinds of celebrity

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:23.000
is the duality of it.

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:24.600
You, as an audience member,
understand

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:27.160
that there is another me
behind this.

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:29.120
We show you
that there is another me.

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:32.320
You know that this
presentation I'm making,

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:34.960
whilst it is utterly
a truthful expression

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:36.680
of something
that I am feeling,

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:39.520
it is ultimately fake.

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:41.120
You know
that I take all this off

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:42.520
at the end of the day.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:44.200
And if you know
that I do that

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:46.480
and I can be given praise

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:49.600
for looking either, whether
it's spectacularly beautiful

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:52.760
or weird or ugly or strange,

00:16:52.840 --> 00:16:54.800
then you know
that none of that stuff

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:56.280
really matters, right?

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:57.880
It's actually OK for you

00:16:57.960 --> 00:16:59.720
to not worry
about the way that you look,

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:01.080
walking down the street.

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:03.280
So I think
it frustrates me sometimes

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:07.960
when people treat drag
as a new beauty craze

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:10.680
rather than
the end of beauty crazes.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:22.840
Scotland is always
the underdog in the UK.

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:24.360
I think people don't take us
that seriously.

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:26.960
They don't kind of think
that we have drag.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:28.000
I think people are amazed

00:17:28.080 --> 00:17:30.120
when they find out there is
drag here, in Scotland.

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:31.480
But the drag scene
here's incredible.

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:32.600
And I think it's so amazing

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:34.480
that we now have
the confirmation of that

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:36.360
through a Drag Race winner
in Lawrence Chaney

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:37.720
who is someone

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:39.920
who started drag here, in…
in, um, Scotland

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.280
and rose through the scene,
became top of the scene

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:43.720
and then won Drag Race.

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:47.040
Testing, testing. Mic check.

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:49.520
One, two, one, two.
Three, four, five.

00:17:49.600 --> 00:17:51.200
Six, seven, eight.

00:17:51.280 --> 00:17:52.960
Nine, ten.
I think we're good.

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:55.000
The Polo Lounge has been
around for, I think,

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:57.680
30-odd years
here in Glasgow.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:17:58.880
It's this gorgeous,
huge building,

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:00.560
right in the Merchant City

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:03.520
and when I started coming
out in the queer scene

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:05.560
when I turned 18,
I would come here,

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:07.640
because it was
the most well-known club.

00:18:07.720 --> 00:18:09.120
I think it's time
we have a nosey round,

00:18:09.200 --> 00:18:10.200
don't you?

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:12.520
Let's go. Come on.

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:35.760
Oh, my God.
That's right, bitches.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:37.960
It's you, it's me, it's us,
and we're back.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:41.840
We are here in Club X
at the Polo Lounge.

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:43.160
And baby…

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:46.840
You like what you see?
You want to get whipped?

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:48.000
You want to get spanked?

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:49.600
Well, honey, you can get
all of that here.

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:51.480
But let's do
a little tour du jour

00:18:51.560 --> 00:18:52.600
and have a good time.

00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:54.280
Follow me.

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:55.720
Right over here,

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:57.720
I really don't know
what's happening, but…

00:18:57.800 --> 00:18:58.600
Oh!

00:19:00.720 --> 00:19:02.280
I don't know what's
happening to be honest,

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:04.280
but I'm getting
a kind of, like…

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:09.840
shoe-shine-y, foot fetish,

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:13.040
licking toe jam
kind of vibe.

00:19:13.120 --> 00:19:15.440
Right. Now.

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:18.080
If you look down
about ankle level,

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:21.000
you will see
a big slab of wood.

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:22.360
And I'm gonna
walk it for you.

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:24.240
Are you ready? Here we go.

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:27.960
Drink in hand and all,
because she's what?

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:29.800
A professional.

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:32.160
And then we take it
down to the cage.

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:34.440
Oh yes, baby. That's right.

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:37.240
You wanna get in?
You want to get locked up?

00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:39.840
You want me to throw away
the key, maybe swallow it?

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:41.240
I could do

00:19:41.720 --> 00:19:43.080
for a price.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:44.400
But mama,

00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:45.960
this is

00:19:46.520 --> 00:19:47.320
Club X

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:49.440
at the Polo Lounge.

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:51.480
Be here, be queer,

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:53.040
and if you're lucky,
I might come around

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:54.600
and spank you some time.

00:19:54.680 --> 00:19:55.520
Yeah.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:57.880
I think the best part
about Polo is

00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:00.720
when you come here, you can
be who you want to be.

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:03.440
Like, you don't have
to kind of hide anything.

00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:05.640
So if you want to come here

00:20:05.720 --> 00:20:07.920
and you want to wear
all leather, for example,

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:09.240
no one's gonna judge you.

00:20:09.320 --> 00:20:10.400
If you want
to come here in drag,

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:11.640
no one's gonna judge you.

00:20:11.720 --> 00:20:13.240
Um, if you want to come here
and wear no top,

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:14.280
no one's gonna judge you.

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:16.760
So it's…
You can just be yourself.

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:20.240
It's you, it's me, it's us,
it's Tia Kofi, it's Netflix,

00:20:20.320 --> 00:20:22.640
it's I Like to Watch, bitch.

00:20:23.600 --> 00:20:25.480
I think, before lockdown,

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:27.640
what was the queer scene
like in Glasgow?

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:28.520
It was

00:20:29.560 --> 00:20:32.640
very white male orientated,
I believe.

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:36.040
Um, even here in Polo
it was very like that, um…

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:38.520
A lot of, like,
the advertisement stuff

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:41.760
was more pushed towards
sort of white, gay men.

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:43.000
And I feel,
having 16 months off,

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:44.280
we really
thought about it more.

00:20:44.360 --> 00:20:45.240
We sat and had chats.

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:46.680
"How can we
make this more accepting?"

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:48.920
And then we brought in
the whole idea of, like,

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:50.040
well why is it just gay men?

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:50.960
Let's make it just queer.

00:20:51.040 --> 00:20:53.280
It's an umbrella term.
It's an inclusive term.

00:20:53.360 --> 00:20:54.960
You're gonna see things
in here

00:20:55.040 --> 00:20:57.200
that you may not see
in straight venues,

00:20:57.280 --> 00:20:58.120
and you're gonna realise

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:00.080
that actually, the gay
community is made up

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:01.680
of loads
of tiny little things,

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:03.560
and it's not just these
set-in-stone,

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:06.240
what you see
throughout the years, on TV.

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:09.640
The future
of Scottish drag is…

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:11.680
I hope, personally,
it's gonna be more accepting

00:21:11.760 --> 00:21:12.640
for

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:14.240
more AFAB queens.

00:21:14.320 --> 00:21:15.560
Definitely it's come in,

00:21:15.640 --> 00:21:17.880
um, and more drag kings.

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:19.400
That's what we're missing
a lot of here.

00:21:19.480 --> 00:21:21.520
It's doesn't just have to be
men dressed as women.

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:23.360
That's…
Drag isn't men anymore.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:25.680
It's not just
a concept of drag queens,

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:27.160
it's a drag artist.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:28.640
So I think that "queen" part

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:30.000
needs to kind of
come out now,

00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:31.680
and we just put "artist"
in there

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:33.920
and make it an open term,
just like the word queer.

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:35.120
It's an umbrella term.

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:37.000
Like, what is drag,
do you know what I mean?

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:39.360
It's art.
It's just a bit of fun.

00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:41.520
So why does it have to be
staggering to just men?

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:47.120
My name's Brent Would,
W-O-U-L-D,

00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:49.520
because I would anything,
literally, especially you.

00:21:55.640 --> 00:21:57.840
Well, I've been dancing
since the age of three.

00:21:57.920 --> 00:21:59.760
That's what I started off
doing: tap, ballet, jazz.

00:21:59.840 --> 00:22:01.320
and singing, because
my family were publicans

00:22:01.400 --> 00:22:02.320
in Wandsworth
and Battersea.

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:05.240
So we're quite a theatrical
family as it is.

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:07.560
And then,
probably about 2016,

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:09.160
I took part
in the Man Up competition

00:22:09.720 --> 00:22:10.680
for drag kings,

00:22:10.760 --> 00:22:12.960
headed up by Adam All
and Apple Derrieres,

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:15.720
who are absolutely fantastic
community leaders

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:16.720
and performers.

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:18.680
Uh, and so, yeah, that was
where I really got into…

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:20.720
into "drag king-ing",
I should say.

00:22:20.800 --> 00:22:23.520
What I find challenging
about drag is

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:24.880
when I've been booked
on line-ups

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:26.200
and I definitely feel
like I'm there

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:27.800
as a tokenistic gesture.

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:31.360
I feel sometimes that I'm
in these environments

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:33.480
whereby I have to try
and perform

00:22:33.560 --> 00:22:35.080
in a way that other people
want me to,

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:36.480
and I don't get
the same reception.

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:38.520
So, I used to do stand-up
for years, just…

00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:39.560
not in drag.

00:22:39.640 --> 00:22:41.320
And often
I would get introduced

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:43.440
as, um, a female comedian,

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:44.880
which often puts you
on the back foot,

00:22:44.960 --> 00:22:46.880
because people are, like,
"Mm, what you gonna do?"

00:22:46.960 --> 00:22:49.000
And then, like, if you fail,
you fail for every woman.

00:22:49.080 --> 00:22:50.440
Drag kings are
at the forefront

00:22:50.520 --> 00:22:52.000
of the radical nature
of drag.

00:22:52.080 --> 00:22:53.160
But drag in and of itself

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:55.040
is like a radical,
political act, right?

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:56.480
And, you know,
just at Stonewall

00:22:56.560 --> 00:22:58.400
when it was a trans woman
who was at the front

00:22:58.480 --> 00:23:00.440
and lesbians that were,
like, very key,

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:01.760
again, people have been
sidelined.

00:23:01.840 --> 00:23:03.080
And I think
we shouldn't forget

00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:05.320
that there's
an entire community of drag

00:23:05.400 --> 00:23:06.640
that we're not seeing
represented

00:23:06.720 --> 00:23:07.760
in the mainstream media.

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:17.480
I want to be able to perform

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:20.360
to audiences who have never
seen a drag king before.

00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:21.840
I think that's amazing.

00:23:21.920 --> 00:23:22.840
First of all, like,

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:25.000
I… I remember the first time
I saw my drag…

00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:26.800
first drag king
and drag queen,

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:28.240
not at the same time.

00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:31.440
But it's a really fun, like,
experience, you know?

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:32.920
What is this, you know?

00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:35.480
I want to tell people that,
you know?

00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:37.920
And just spread the…
the word that there's more

00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:39.920
than just drag queens
out there.

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:42.840
There's, like, all kinds
of different drag creatures.

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:45.200
It's just kind of getting
gender equality here.

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:46.680
It's like, well, why is it

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:49.800
that we can't do
the same as drag queens?

00:23:50.280 --> 00:23:51.120
You know?

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:57.440
When I started drag,
like, 14 years ago nearly,

00:23:57.520 --> 00:23:59.440
yeah, oh my God, 14 years,

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:02.480
uh, it was kind of… it was
a very underground art form.

00:24:02.560 --> 00:24:04.600
We were the kind of weirdos
of the scene.

00:24:04.680 --> 00:24:05.520
You know, we did drag.

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:07.280
Everyone loved us
in the bar,

00:24:07.360 --> 00:24:09.600
but then in the day,
no one knew who we were,

00:24:09.680 --> 00:24:10.600
which was kind of nice

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:12.600
because you have
this kind of alter ego.

00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:13.760
But at the same time,

00:24:13.840 --> 00:24:15.480
you know, nobody wanted
to date a drag queen,

00:24:15.560 --> 00:24:17.640
nobody wanted to be friends
with a drag queen.

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:20.360
But now, drag is everywhere.

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:22.160
It's all over the TV,
it's in theatres,

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:23.800
there's brunches,
there's bars.

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:26.240
I mean, it is everywhere.

00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:28.360
You know, a lot of Drag Race
fans will have never heard

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:30.720
of Lily Savage, Danny
La Rue, Charles Pierce,

00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:33.360
Jimmy James,
legends of drag.

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:35.880
And there is kind of a…

00:24:35.960 --> 00:24:37.640
I don't want to say,
like, a wall

00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:40.760
between older drag queens
and younger drag queens.

00:24:40.840 --> 00:24:43.000
It's very slight.

00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:43.880
But sometimes,

00:24:43.960 --> 00:24:45.040
I'm… it's just happening.

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:47.200
You know, things…
things blow up

00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:50.160
and underground things
become mainstream.

00:24:50.240 --> 00:24:52.240
So I came up
with the name Stacey Recht

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:54.440
because I always
found myself funny.

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:57.560
Not to be big-headed, but I
do find myself hilarious.

00:24:57.640 --> 00:25:00.000
And I was searching
on the internet one day

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:01.400
for, like, pun names,

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:04.360
and it come up and it just,
like, shone out to me.

00:25:04.440 --> 00:25:07.400
And I thought, "That is me.
I am Stacey Recht."

00:25:07.480 --> 00:25:09.400
So, I am 20 years old.

00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:11.600
I've been doing drag
for two years.

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:12.800
Um…

00:25:13.520 --> 00:25:16.400
Half of it was spent
during, like, the pandemic

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:18.080
and the lockdowns,

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:21.080
but, um, I used that time

00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:24.000
to, like,
just to focus on my craft.

00:25:24.080 --> 00:25:27.000
Like, I learned how to sew.
I learned, you know…

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:28.720
I learned more makeup skills

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:31.280
to make myself
look even prettier.

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:33.440
I'm a queen for photos.

00:25:33.520 --> 00:25:35.920
Like, I love memories
that you can hold,

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:36.920
like this photo.

00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:39.000
Um, I've got a signed photo
of The Vivienne.

00:25:39.080 --> 00:25:41.000
It's a special place
in my heart

00:25:41.080 --> 00:25:42.800
because it was
the first ever gig

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:45.160
I ever did outside
of my hometown.

00:25:45.240 --> 00:25:46.720
And I had to win
a drag competition

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:49.760
just to, like, open for her.

00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:51.800
And I remember meeting her,
and I was like, transfixed.

00:25:51.880 --> 00:25:54.760
I was, like, "Wow.
Like, she won Drag Race."

00:25:54.840 --> 00:25:57.200
I was just, like, so in awe.

00:25:57.280 --> 00:26:00.120
And she was so lovely
and so, like, humble.

00:26:00.200 --> 00:26:02.360
And I had my crown on
from winning the competition

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:03.240
and she was, like,

00:26:03.320 --> 00:26:04.440
"Congratulations
on your win."

00:26:04.520 --> 00:26:05.520
I was, like,
"Congratulations

00:26:05.600 --> 00:26:06.680
on yours, too.

00:26:06.760 --> 00:26:08.920
Your crown's probably
a lot better than mine."

00:26:09.480 --> 00:26:11.360
Being from a small town,

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:13.440
you've got
to fight that hard…

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:15.200
Like, you've got
to fight harder

00:26:15.280 --> 00:26:16.600
for the opportunities.

00:26:16.680 --> 00:26:19.800
Problems or, like, hardships
that come with drag

00:26:19.880 --> 00:26:20.920
is, like, money.

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:22.160
Like, I spend, like,

00:26:22.240 --> 00:26:25.000
every penny that I make in…

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:26.040
going into, like, my drag.

00:26:26.120 --> 00:26:28.800
Everything gets funnelled
back into Stacey.

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:32.360
And sometimes, like,
Lewis, like, as a boy,

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:35.640
gets left out because
I put her to the front.

00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:37.400
But she is the one
that makes the money.

00:26:37.480 --> 00:26:39.960
And it's trying to find
that, like, that balance.

00:26:40.720 --> 00:26:43.680
Some queens just don't
have the funds to, like,

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:47.360
get extravagant, like,
extravagant costumes,

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:48.440
like, you know.

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:51.520
Some of them have to resort
to, you know, wearing, like,

00:26:51.600 --> 00:26:54.600
a £5 dress from,
like, Primark or stuff.

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:55.760
And

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:58.400
what winds me up is, like,
people see, like, Drag Race

00:26:58.480 --> 00:27:00.400
and see these amazing
outfits and they think,

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:02.560
"Oh, so all drag queens
should wear that."

00:27:02.640 --> 00:27:03.480
And it's, like, "No.

00:27:03.560 --> 00:27:05.600
That's what you should wear
on Drag Race."

00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:08.640
Like, I know Veronica
mentioned on Instagram

00:27:08.720 --> 00:27:11.040
after her Drag Race
elimination

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:13.400
how, like, she was
struggling for money

00:27:13.480 --> 00:27:15.720
after… because
of the Covid pandemic,

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:18.160
and then having to do
season 2, then season 3.

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:20.960
Like, just trying to find
the funds and stuff.

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:23.040
And I think people forget

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:24.880
the money that goes into it.

00:27:24.960 --> 00:27:26.320
And they also forget, like,

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:28.200
the… just the pure work,

00:27:28.280 --> 00:27:30.680
like, that goes
into getting into drag.

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:33.840
Like, people think you only
work, like, five, six hours.

00:27:33.920 --> 00:27:35.800
But I do, like,
12 hours a day

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:37.120
just coming in
and getting ready

00:27:37.200 --> 00:27:38.200
and then getting
out of drag.

00:27:38.280 --> 00:27:40.600
Like, I spend more time
in this dressing room

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:43.320
than I do
at my own house sometimes.

00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:45.280
But I wouldn't trade it
for the world.

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:47.760
I honest… Honestly, I
don't know what I'd be doing

00:27:47.840 --> 00:27:48.920
without drag, like.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:50.400
I'd say drag saved my life,

00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:53.160
because before drag,
I was a bit lost,

00:27:53.240 --> 00:27:55.800
but now I feel
like I've got a purpose.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:58.320
Now, guys.

00:27:58.880 --> 00:28:00.880
We have had
the pleasure of having

00:28:00.960 --> 00:28:03.840
one of the most
fabulous divas of Hull

00:28:03.920 --> 00:28:05.240
join us tonight.

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:08.520
Stacey Recht!

00:28:08.600 --> 00:28:11.760
Make some noise for Stacey!

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:19.920
Drag Race UK has created

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:22.520
opportunities
for queer performers

00:28:22.600 --> 00:28:23.800
and for drag artists

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:25.600
that never existed before.

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:26.480
It's a very…

00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:28.120
It's done an amazing
and a wonderful thing.

00:28:28.200 --> 00:28:29.120
And I'm so excited

00:28:29.200 --> 00:28:31.320
to see that
that door is now opening

00:28:31.400 --> 00:28:34.120
to female-identifying
performers,

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:35.960
to AFAB queens,
to trans queens.

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:37.560
It's wonderful to see
that happening as well,

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:38.880
because that diversity
has always been

00:28:38.960 --> 00:28:40.440
in our scenes.

00:28:40.520 --> 00:28:42.720
Um, I think
what comes with that,

00:28:42.800 --> 00:28:44.800
and what always comes
hand-in-hand with that,

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:46.560
is that you have one hell
of a lot more people

00:28:46.640 --> 00:28:47.640
doing drag.

00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:49.200
And in many ways,

00:28:49.720 --> 00:28:52.400
the way that you see drag
working now in the UK,

00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:55.280
it resembles the wider model
of reality television,

00:28:55.360 --> 00:28:57.920
and then the wider model
that we have in our society

00:28:58.000 --> 00:28:59.960
of even unregulated,
free-market capitalism.

00:29:00.040 --> 00:29:00.840
You know what I mean?

00:29:00.920 --> 00:29:01.920
The people at the very top,

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:03.480
the people
at the apex of the pyramid,

00:29:03.560 --> 00:29:06.200
become the incentive
for all of those people

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:08.680
who you have
creating art underneath.

00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:10.000
And whilst that is

00:29:10.080 --> 00:29:11.000
a great thing

00:29:11.080 --> 00:29:13.200
that it becomes
an incentive to create,

00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:14.960
you're gonna have a lot
of people disappointed.

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:16.760
And so whenever
I meet new drag queens,

00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:18.200
and they…
people ask me, like,

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:19.840
"What is
your big tip for drag?

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:22.440
How do I become
successful in drag?"

00:29:22.520 --> 00:29:24.200
I always say,
"Have something else."

00:29:24.280 --> 00:29:26.480
For two reasons:
you'll need to survive,

00:29:26.560 --> 00:29:29.160
and not everybody can be
on the show.

00:29:29.240 --> 00:29:31.280
Not everybody
can make it big,

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:33.320
and that's the way
that it's set up.

00:29:33.400 --> 00:29:36.320
But also, because it makes
your drag more interesting.

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:41.000
Hull, make some noise

00:29:41.080 --> 00:29:47.160
for the superstar
that is Miss Stacey Recht!

00:29:49.520 --> 00:29:50.880
Isn't that exciting?

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:52.120
Sorry, I did that thing

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:53.240
where I looked
at the camera.

00:29:53.320 --> 00:29:55.160
Is that gonna be awful?

00:29:57.640 --> 00:29:58.800
Stacey, give me a clap

00:29:58.880 --> 00:30:00.040
in front of your face,
would you?

00:30:02.920 --> 00:30:04.920
I'm a proper director now.

00:30:06.680 --> 00:30:08.880
What's the future of Polo
Lounge? Another 25 years.

00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:10.040
That's what I hope.
Even more.

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:12.880
That's my biggest hope, that
it stands for a long time

00:30:12.960 --> 00:30:14.360
and that it constantly moves
with the times

00:30:14.440 --> 00:30:15.720
and it constantly
updates itself

00:30:15.800 --> 00:30:17.800
and it constantly keeps
regenerating,

00:30:17.880 --> 00:30:19.720
because that's
where queer venues

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:20.840
need to focus on right now,

00:30:20.920 --> 00:30:22.880
is the constant change
that's happening.

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:24.000
When it comes
to new pronouns

00:30:24.080 --> 00:30:25.000
and all this kind of stuff,

00:30:25.080 --> 00:30:26.280
you need to stay up to date.

00:30:26.360 --> 00:30:27.600
You can't stay in the past.

00:30:27.680 --> 00:30:29.440
Like, you need to walk
with the community.

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:31.760
I really hope that Polo
continues to do that

00:30:32.480 --> 00:30:34.560
and creates a safe space
for a long time to come.

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:37.000
At its heart,
drag is queer expression.

00:30:37.080 --> 00:30:39.480
It's not meant to be
necessarily palatable.

00:30:39.560 --> 00:30:41.720
It's not meant
to be beautiful or, um…

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:43.440
It's not…
it's not meant to, in a way,

00:30:43.520 --> 00:30:45.280
please straight people.

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:47.200
What I think really
characterises UK drag

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:50.080
is that anarchic, punk,
pantomimic sense.

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:51.160
I feel

00:30:51.240 --> 00:30:53.240
like we're not always
polished, look-wise.

00:30:53.320 --> 00:30:55.520
So I think that performance
and comedy

00:30:55.600 --> 00:30:57.200
and not taking yourself
too seriously,

00:30:57.280 --> 00:30:58.160
but quite a punky nature,

00:30:58.240 --> 00:31:01.320
is the heart of UK drag
for me.

00:31:01.400 --> 00:31:03.200
We're queer storytellers.

00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:06.240
Our history comes from being
in a marginalised community.

00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:09.440
Our purpose, if you like,
is to amplify,

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:12.720
um, people
who are not amplified.

00:31:12.800 --> 00:31:14.040
Drag Race and…

00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:16.880
It… It's given us a platform
that we can either

00:31:17.480 --> 00:31:18.760
sit on and enjoy

00:31:18.840 --> 00:31:21.240
or we can invite
other people onto it,

00:31:21.320 --> 00:31:23.640
and other people
can use that as well.

00:31:23.720 --> 00:31:26.080
Drag Race queens are
very little, really.

00:31:26.160 --> 00:31:27.400
We've had three seasons
in the UK.

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:29.120
What's that, 30 queens?

00:31:29.200 --> 00:31:31.280
There's plenty room
for everyone else.

00:31:31.360 --> 00:31:34.920
I think the general public
have a more expansive view

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:38.040
about what drag is,
who can do drag

00:31:38.120 --> 00:31:39.080
and all of that.

00:31:39.160 --> 00:31:42.280
Shoutout to Victoria Scone,
changing the world.

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:44.560
I think the idea of drag

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:47.040
being this stale, kind of,
"dressed-as-a-girl",

00:31:47.120 --> 00:31:49.360
only drag queens
kind of idea

00:31:49.440 --> 00:31:53.080
is something that
maybe existed in the past.

00:31:53.160 --> 00:31:55.320
But as we go forward,
I think we're gonna start

00:31:55.400 --> 00:31:57.840
seeing
way more playing around

00:31:57.920 --> 00:32:00.800
with not just, like,
femininity, but masculinity,

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:02.000
gender-blurring,

00:32:02.080 --> 00:32:05.360
gender queer, kind of,
gender fluid identities.

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:08.280
Um, so many performers
are non-binary, you know,

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:11.280
and that's a really integral
part of, like, who they are

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:13.160
and how they express
themselves.

00:32:13.240 --> 00:32:15.160
And so I think
it's just gonna kind of go

00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:16.320
in all directions.

00:32:16.400 --> 00:32:19.320
It is punk rock.
It is political.

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:21.440
It is about, um,

00:32:21.520 --> 00:32:24.200
questioning the status quo
and standing up

00:32:24.280 --> 00:32:26.320
to heteronormative values
in our world.

00:32:26.400 --> 00:32:28.720
Um, I think if we lose that,

00:32:28.800 --> 00:32:31.160
drag will kind of lose
its meaning.

00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:33.600
So I hope that
that is always remembered

00:32:33.680 --> 00:32:37.280
as drag continues to grow
and take over the world.

